To solve this problem, we need to iterate through each number in the array and determine if it is prime or not. A prime number is a positive integer greater than 1 that has no positive divisors other than 1 and itself. 

We can create a helper function `isPrime` that takes an integer as input and returns true if it is prime and false otherwise. The function will check if the number is divisible by any integer from 2 to the square root of the number. If it is divisible by any of these integers, then it is not prime.

Using the `isPrime` helper function, we can iterate through the input array and keep track of the prime numbers using a separate array. We initialize an empty array `primeNumbers` and then iterate through each number in the input array. If the number is prime, we add it to the `primeNumbers` array.

Finally, we return the `primeNumbers` array.

Here is the updated code:

```javascript
function isPrime(num: number): boolean {
  if (num < 2) {
    return false;
  }

  for (let i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(num); i++) {
    if (num % i === 0) {
      return false;
    }
  }

  return true;
}

function filterPrimeNumbers(arr: number[]): number[] {
  const primeNumbers = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (isPrime(arr[i])) {
      primeNumbers.push(arr[i]);
    }
  }

  return primeNumbers;
}
```

The time complexity of this solution is O(n * sqrt(m)), where n is the length of the input array and m is the maximum value in the array. The space complexity is O(k), where k is the number of prime numbers in the array.

