```java
import java.util.Comparator;

interface CustomInterface {
    // method to be implemented by custom class
    void customMethod();
}

class CustomClass implements CustomInterface {
    private int value;

    public CustomClass(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    public int getValue() {
        return value;
    }

    @Override
    public void customMethod() {
        System.out.println("Performing a specific operation on object with value: " + value);
    }
}

class CustomComparator implements Comparator<CustomClass> {
    private boolean ascending;

    public CustomComparator(boolean ascending) {
        this.ascending = ascending;
    }

    @Override
    public int compare(CustomClass obj1, CustomClass obj2) {
        if (ascending) {
            return Integer.compare(obj1.getValue(), obj2.getValue());
        } else {
            return Integer.compare(obj2.getValue(), obj1.getValue());
        }
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static <T extends CustomInterface> void bubbleSort(T[] array, Comparator<T> comparator) {
        int n = array.length;
        boolean swapped;

        for (int i = 0; i < n - 1; i++) {
            swapped = false;

            for (int j = 0; j < n - i - 1; j++) {
                if (comparator.compare(array[j], array[j + 1]) > 0) {
                    T temp = array[j];
                    array[j] = array[j + 1];
                    array[j + 1] = temp;
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }

            if (!swapped) {
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int N = 5; // size of the array

        CustomClass[] array = new CustomClass[N];
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) {
            array[i] = new CustomClass((int) (Math.random() * 100)); // generating random values for simplicity
        }

        // sorting the array in ascending order
        bubbleSort(array, new CustomComparator(true));

        System.out.println("Sorted array in ascending order:");
        for (CustomClass obj : array) {
            System.out.println(obj.getValue());
        }

        // sorting the array in descending order
        bubbleSort(array, new CustomComparator(false));

        System.out.println("Sorted array in descending order:");
        for (CustomClass obj : array) {
            System.out.println(obj.getValue());
        }

        // performing a specific operation on the sorted array
        for (CustomClass obj : array) {
            obj.customMethod();
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we have a `CustomInterface` that defines the method `customMethod()` to be implemented by the custom class. The `CustomClass` implements this interface and overrides the method. It also has a `getValue()` method for comparison purposes.

We have a `CustomComparator` class that implements the `Comparator` interface and compares `CustomClass` objects based on their values. It also takes a boolean parameter `ascending` to determine whether to sort in ascending or descending order.

The `bubbleSort()` method performs the bubble sort algorithm on the generic array `T[]` using the provided `Comparator`. The method uses the `compare()` method of the `Comparator` to determine the order of elements.

In the `main()` method, we create an array of `CustomClass` objects with random values. We first sort the array in ascending order using `bubbleSort()` and a `CustomComparator` with `ascending` set to `true`. We then print the sorted array and sort it again in descending order using `bubbleSort()` and a `CustomComparator` with `ascending` set to `false`. Finally, we perform a specific operation (`customMethod()`) on each object in the sorted array.

