To find the minimum number in an array of integers in Java without using any built-in sorting or min() function, and ensuring a time complexity of O(n log n), you can use the divide and conquer approach. Here is an example implementation:

```java
public class MinimumNumberFinder {

    public static int findMinimum(int[] arr) {
        return findMinimumHelper(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
    }

    private static int findMinimumHelper(int[] arr, int start, int end) {
        if (start == end) {
            return arr[start];
        }

        int mid = (start + end) / 2;
        int leftMin = findMinimumHelper(arr, start, mid);
        int rightMin = findMinimumHelper(arr, mid + 1, end);

        return Math.min(leftMin, rightMin);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {5, 2, 9, 1, 7};
        int minimum = findMinimum(arr);
        System.out.println("Minimum number: " + minimum);
    }
}
```

In this implementation, the `findMinimum` method is the entry point which calls the `findMinimumHelper` method recursively. The `findMinimumHelper` method uses divide and conquer by splitting the array into two halves and recursively finding the minimum of each half. Finally, it returns the minimum of the left half and the right half using the `Math.min` function.

By dividing the array into halves at each recursive call, the time complexity of this algorithm is O(n log n).

