Here's a code in Java that finds all prime numbers between 0 and n, with a time complexity of O(nlog(logn)). It also handles cases where n is negative or zero by displaying an error message:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class PrimeNumbers {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 100; // change the value of n as per your requirement

        // check if n is negative or zero
        if (n <= 0) {
            System.out.println("n must be a positive integer.");
            return;
        }

        // create a boolean array to mark numbers as prime or not
        boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[n + 1];

        // initialize all numbers as prime
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            isPrime[i] = true;
        }

        // sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm
        for (int i = 2; i * i <= n; i++) {
            if (isPrime[i]) {
                for (int j = i * i; j <= n; j += i) {
                    isPrime[j] = false;
                }
            }
        }

        // collect all prime numbers into a list
        ArrayList<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            if (isPrime[i]) {
                primes.add(i);
            }
        }

        // print all prime numbers
        System.out.println("Prime numbers between 0 and " + n + ":");
        for (int prime : primes) {
            System.out.print(prime + " ");
        }
    }
}
```

The code uses the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm to efficiently find prime numbers up to n. It starts by assuming all numbers are prime, and then iteratively marks multiples of prime numbers as non-prime. Finally, it collects all the prime numbers into an ArrayList and prints them.

