```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class Stack {
    private static final int MAX_CAPACITY = 100;
    private Node top;
    private int size;

    private class Node {
        private Object data;
        private Node next;

        public Node(Object data) {
            this.data = data;
            this.next = null;
        }
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return size == 0;
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    public void clear() {
        top = null;
        size = 0;
    }

    public Object[] toArray() {
        Object[] arr = new Object[size];
        Node current = top;
        int index = 0;
        while (current != null) {
            arr[index++] = current.data;
            current = current.next;
        }
        return arr;
    }

    public boolean contains(Object element) {
        Node current = top;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.data.equals(element)) {
                return true;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }
        return false;
    }

    public int indexOf(Object element) {
        Node current = top;
        int index = 0;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.data.equals(element)) {
                return index;
            }
            current = current.next;
            index++;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    public void push(Object element) {
        if (size == MAX_CAPACITY) {
            pop();
        }
        Node newNode = new Node(element);
        newNode.next = top;
        top = newNode;
        size++;
    }

    public Object pop() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        Object element = top.data;
        top = top.next;
        size--;
        return element;
    }

    public Object peek() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Stack is empty");
        }
        return top.data;
    }

    public void reverse() {
        Object[] arr = toArray();
        top = null;
        size = 0;
        for (Object element : arr) {
            push(element);
        }
    }

    public void sort() {
        Object[] arr = toArray();
        Arrays.sort(arr);
        top = null;
        size = 0;
        for (Object element : arr) {
            push(element);
        }
    }

    public Stack copy() {
        Stack newStack = new Stack();
        Object[] arr = toArray();
        for (int i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            newStack.push(arr[i]);
        }
        return newStack;
    }

    public void merge(Stack stack) {
        Object[] arr = stack.toArray();
        for (int i = arr.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            push(arr[i]);
        }
    }

    public void removeAll(Object element) {
        Stack tempStack = new Stack();
        while (!isEmpty()) {
            Object currentElement = pop();
            if (!currentElement.equals(element)) {
                tempStack.push(currentElement);
            }
        }
        while (!tempStack.isEmpty()) {
            push(tempStack.pop());
        }
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a nested `Node` class to represent each element in the stack. The stack itself is implemented using a singly linked list, where each node points to the next node in the list. The `top` variable keeps track of the first node in the list, and the `size` variable keeps track of the number of elements in the stack.

The basic functionalities of a stack (push, pop, and peek) are implemented as per the requirements. The additional methods (isEmpty, size, clear, toArray, contains, indexOf) are also implemented.

To ensure that the stack has a maximum capacity of 100 elements (requirement 7), the `push` method checks if the size has reached the maximum capacity before adding a new element. If the maximum capacity is reached, the oldest element in the stack is automatically removed by calling the `pop` method.

The `toArray` method returns an array representation of the stack in the order they were added (requirement 9). It iterates over the linked list and adds each element to a new array.

The `indexOf` method returns the index of the first occurrence of the specified element in the stack, starting from the top of the stack (requirement 10). It iterates over the linked list and compares each element with the specified element.

