Develop a function to calculate the median of a set of numbers in C#. 

Write a function named "CalculateMedian" that takes in an array of integers and returns the median value. 

The median is defined as the middle value in a sorted list of numbers. If the list has an odd number of elements, the median is the value at the center index. If the list has an even number of elements, the median is the average of the two middle values.

Your function should handle the following cases:
1. An empty array: Return 0 as the median.
2. An array with only one element: Return that element as the median.
3. An array with an odd number of elements: Return the value at the center index as the median.
4. An array with an even number of elements: Return the average of the two middle values as the median.

Your solution should be implemented without using any built-in sorting or mathematical functions.

Example:
Input: [5, 2, 9, 1, 7]
Output: 5

Explanation: The sorted array is [1, 2, 5, 7, 9]. The median is the value at the center index, which is 5.

Note:
- You can assume that the input array will not be null.

Reference Code:
```csharp
public static int CalculateMedian(int[] numbers)
{
    if (numbers.Length == 0)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    if (numbers.Length == 1)
    {
        return numbers[0];
    }

    Array.Sort(numbers);

    int middleIndex = numbers.Length / 2;

    if (numbers.Length % 2 == 0)
    {
        return (numbers[middleIndex] + numbers[middleIndex - 1]) / 2;
    }
    else
    {
        return numbers[middleIndex];
    }
}
```

Here is an alternative implementation of the CalculateMedian function without using any built-in sorting or mathematical functions:

```csharp
public static int CalculateMedian(int[] numbers)
{
    if (numbers.Length == 0)
    {
        return 0;
    }

    if (numbers.Length == 1)
    {
        return numbers[0];
    }

    int[] sortedNumbers = MergeSort(numbers);

    int middleIndex = sortedNumbers.Length / 2;

    if (sortedNumbers.Length % 2 == 0)
    {
        return (sortedNumbers[middleIndex] + sortedNumbers[middleIndex - 1]) / 2;
    }
    else
    {
        return sortedNumbers[middleIndex];
    }
}

public static int[] MergeSort(int[] numbers)
{
    if (numbers.Length <= 1)
    {
        return numbers;
    }

    int middleIndex = numbers.Length / 2;
    int[] left = new int[middleIndex];
    int[] right = new int[numbers.Length - middleIndex];

    Array.Copy(numbers, 0, left, 0, middleIndex);
    Array.Copy(numbers, middleIndex, right, 0, numbers.Length - middleIndex);

    left = MergeSort(left);
    right = MergeSort(right);

    return Merge(left, right);
}

public static int[] Merge(int[] left, int[] right)
{
    int[] merged = new int[left.Length + right.Length];
    int i = 0;
    int j = 0;
    int k = 0;

    while (i < left.Length && j < right.Length)
    {
        if (left[i] <= right[j])
        {
            merged[k] = left[i];
            i++;
        }
        else
        {
            merged[k] = right[j];
            j++;
        }

        k++;
    }

    while (i < left.Length)
    {
        merged[k] = left[i];
        i++;
        k++;
    }

    while (j < right.Length)
    {
        merged[k] = right[j];
        j++;
        k++;
    }

    return merged;
}
```

This implementation uses the merge sort algorithm to sort the input array in ascending order. The sorted array is then used to calculate the median value based on the length and whether it is odd or even. The merge sort algorithm has a time complexity of O(n log n), where n is the number of elements in the input array.

