Here is the implementation of the selection sort algorithm in C:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int* a, int* b) {
    int temp = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = temp;
}

void selectionSort(int arr[], int n) {
    int i, j, maxIndex;

    for (i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
        maxIndex = i;

        // Find the maximum element in the unsorted part of the array
        for (j = i+1; j < n; j++) {
            if (arr[j] > arr[maxIndex]) {
                maxIndex = j;
            }
        }

        // Swap the maximum element with the current element
        swap(&arr[i], &arr[maxIndex]);
    }
}

int main() {
    int nums[] = {5, 2, -3, 10, 23, 99, -1, 7, 93, 0, 17, 64, 48, 72, 36, 100, 9, -5, 81, 55};
    int n = sizeof(nums) / sizeof(nums[0]);

    selectionSort(nums, n);

    printf("Sorted array in descending order: ");
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        printf("%d ", nums[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}
```

Output:
```
Sorted array in descending order: 100 99 93 81 72 64 55 48 36 23 17 10 9 7 5 2 0 -1 -3 -5
```

Explanation:
The selection sort algorithm works by repeatedly finding the maximum element from the unsorted part of the array and swapping it with the current element. This process is repeated until the entire array is sorted in descending order. The time complexity of selection sort is O(n^2) and the auxiliary space complexity is O(1).

